From: Lars Trieloff <lars@trieloff.net>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:08:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/13] set: seal piped values and ask seven's vault on a
 terminal

With a sealed provider, `fnox set NAME` seals a value read from a pipe
(or --from-file) and stores the placeholder under the secret's own
name. With stdin on a terminal it never prompts: it sends `request
{kind: "value"}` so seven's vault dialog takes the value, polls
`status` every second for up to 300 s, and sends `cancel` on Ctrl-C
(SIGINT or SIGTERM are caught through the WASIX signal callback). A
value given as an argument, which other processes can read from
/proc, and --key-name are refused for sealed secrets.

